xnu: select, poll and kevent wait on PureDarwin's linuxkpi file descriptors

pd_lfile's select routine only asked the object whether it was ready and recorded nothing,
so a select()/poll() on a sync_file or dma-buf descriptor that was not ready slept until its
timeout, and kevent refused the descriptor (fo_no_kqfilter).

- one selinfo for all linux files (the object belongs to the linuxkpi side and has no room
  for one), under a mutex that is the selinfo's "primitive lock"; pd_lfile_select()
  records on it before asking the object, so a wakeup between the two is not lost;
- pd_lfile_wakeup(obj), exported with the other pd_lfile entry points: the linuxkpi side
  calls it when an object's state changed (a fence signalled). Every waiter then asks its
  own object again; the lock is not held across calls into the object's ops, so the
  linuxkpi side may call it with its own locks held;
- a kevent filter, EVFILTID_PDLFILE, for EVFILT_READ and EVFILT_WRITE: the selspec scheme
  spec_filtops uses for select-only devices (run select with the record hook, selwakeup()
  activates and detaches the knote, the process routine asks again and re-attaches).

A wakeup is not per object: with many linux files open, each wakeup makes every waiter
poll once. Precise wakeups need a selinfo per file, i.e. storage the kernel owns per
fileglob; not done.
UPSTREAM: yes.

